// Original educational copy. Search themes are documented in research/CONTENT_KEYWORDS_2026-10-12.md.
// Modules are shared only when the same practical guidance applies to more than one page.
const sections = {
  brief: ['Prepare a clear writing brief', `Before opening an AI cover letter generator, write down the reader, the opportunity and the action you want the reader to take. A useful brief might say: “I am applying for a junior analyst role at a logistics company. I want the hiring team to see how my spreadsheet work and customer support experience match their reporting responsibilities.” This gives the draft a direction. A list of adjectives such as motivated, innovative and passionate gives it much less useful evidence. Keep the actual vacancy open alongside the generator so you can check every important detail.

The writing workspace separates your background from the opportunity and your preferred tone. Use that separation deliberately. Put your real skills and experience in the personal details, the vacancy or programme information in the opportunity fields, and length or style requirements in the custom instructions. If a detail is unknown, ask the model to leave it out. Never ask it to manufacture a qualification or a result. The best starting point is a short, accurate collection of facts that you could comfortably discuss in an interview.`, '/dashboard'],
  evidence: ['Turn experience into useful evidence', `A strong letter explains what you did, the situation in which you did it and why it matters to this reader. Instead of “I have excellent communication skills,” describe how you answered recurring customer questions, organised a handover or explained a technical issue to a colleague. Numbers help when you can verify them, but they are optional. A precise description without a percentage is more credible than a dramatic metric you cannot support. Projects, volunteering, coursework and responsibilities outside paid work can also provide relevant examples.

Try making a three-column note before generating: requirement, evidence, connection. For a role asking for attention to detail, your evidence might be checking weekly inventory records; the connection is that you can identify inconsistencies before they reach a customer. Include two or three strong connections rather than every task you have ever completed. After generation, check that the draft preserves the scale of your contribution. “Helped prepare” must not quietly become “led the department.” You should recognise your own experience in every paragraph.`, '/guides'],
  job: ['Tailor a cover letter to the job description', `A job description contains several kinds of information: the employer’s priorities, daily responsibilities, required qualifications and preferred experience. Read them separately. Start with the responsibilities you can realistically perform, then identify the evidence that supports your application. A tailored cover letter does not need to repeat the full advertisement. It needs to show the reader how your background connects to the most relevant parts of it. Check the role title, organisation name, location and employment type before you copy any generated text into an application.

When a vacancy is provided by URL or screenshot, confirm that the information is current and readable. A private portal, an expired listing or an image with missing text can produce an incomplete analysis. If the result looks vague, add the important requirements yourself in the instructions instead of repeatedly requesting another draft. For multiple applications, keep a separate version for each employer. Changing only the company name leaves a generic letter; changing the examples and the reason for applying creates a meaningful adaptation.`, '/dashboard'],
  structure: ['Build a letter that is easy to read', `A practical job application letter usually has an introduction, one or two evidence paragraphs and a brief close. The introduction identifies the opportunity and gives a specific reason for applying. The evidence paragraphs connect your experience to the work. The close expresses interest in the next step without demanding an interview. This is a useful starting structure, not a universal rule. Follow the employer’s instructions if they ask for a particular format or a response to selection criteria.

Keep each paragraph focused on one idea. If a paragraph covers your degree, every previous employer, a hobby and three unrelated skills, split it or remove the weaker material. Avoid using the same claim in the opening and conclusion. When you read the draft aloud, listen for sentences that you would never naturally say. Replace abstract phrases with the actual work you have done. The editor lets you revise the generated text directly, so the first response can serve as a scaffold rather than a final document. A clear, honest letter is easier for a reader to assess than a densely decorated one.`, '/guides'],
  beginner: ['Write with limited professional experience', `An entry-level cover letter should make your available experience understandable, rather than disguise its limits. A student can describe a course project, a group assignment, volunteering, a part-time role or an independent learning task. Explain your contribution and what it taught you. If you tested an idea, researched a topic or coordinated a small event, those activities can show useful habits. State the setting accurately so the employer can distinguish academic work from professional employment.

For example, a graduate applying for an operations role might connect a scheduling task in a student society to the vacancy’s planning responsibilities. The point is not that the two settings are identical; it is that the candidate has a concrete example of organising information and following through. Avoid apologising throughout the letter for being new. Identify the skills you already bring, acknowledge any required learning honestly and give a specific reason for choosing the opportunity. In the generator, concise factual background often works better than a long request to make an inexperienced candidate sound like a senior expert.`, '/guides'],
  career: ['Explain a career change without a generic story', `A career change letter works best when it explains the connection between your previous work and the new role. A customer service professional moving into account coordination may bring experience handling enquiries, documenting follow-ups and keeping information accurate. A teacher moving into training design may have examples of planning learning materials and adapting explanations. Identify those bridges before you draft. A new job title does not erase the value of your earlier work, but it also does not automatically make every past task relevant.

Use the custom instructions to describe your transition in plain language. Include why you are changing direction, which skills transfer and what preparation you have completed. If you have not yet used a tool professionally, distinguish a training project from workplace experience. After generation, check that the introduction explains the move without becoming an autobiography. The reader needs enough context to understand the application and enough evidence to assess your fit. Save the employer-specific version separately so you can adjust the emphasis for other opportunities without overwriting a useful draft.`, '/dashboard'],
  motivation: ['Prepare a university motivation letter', `A university motivation letter has a different purpose from a job cover letter. The admissions reader wants to understand your academic interests, preparation, reasons for choosing the programme and possible direction after study. Begin with the actual prompt from the university. Some institutions ask for a statement of purpose, others for a personal statement or answers to specific questions. These documents can overlap, but their instructions should decide your structure and length. Do not assume that a general template meets every application requirement.

In the workspace, select the university purpose and provide the programme name together with relevant background. Explain what you want to study and what experience led you to that interest. A course, project, reading topic or practical problem can make the explanation more specific. Avoid claims about professors, modules or facilities unless you have checked them on an official page. Your letter should connect your preparation to the programme, rather than praise the university in broad terms. Follow the institution’s policy on AI assistance and make sure the final statement is a truthful account of your own goals.`, '/guides'],
  programme: ['Connect your goals to the programme', `Specific programme fit is more useful than a paragraph about an institution being prestigious. Look for the curriculum, learning outcomes, entry requirements and application instructions on the official programme website. Identify the parts that genuinely relate to your interests. A relevant module, a research area or a teaching approach can be a useful connection, but only if you understand it and can explain why it matters. Avoid naming academic staff simply because their names appeared in a search result.

Write a short note with three parts: what you have studied or done, what you want to investigate next, and how this programme could help. For instance, experience analysing a small community survey might lead to an interest in better research methods. The connection is meaningful because the previous activity explains the next learning goal. Check that the generated draft does not turn an exploratory interest into a claim of expert research. For several universities, keep the personal background consistent while changing the programme-specific reasoning. Review every version against that institution’s current prompt before submitting it.`, '/dashboard'],
  research: ['Check university research against official sources', `University information can change between application cycles. Deadlines, tuition, scholarship conditions, document requirements and programme availability should be confirmed on the institution’s own website. An AI research summary is a convenient starting point for organising information, but it is not an official admissions decision. Open the source links, check the academic year and note whether the information applies to your nationality, qualification level and study mode. If a summary has no source for an important claim, treat that claim as unresolved.

Use research notes to record the programme URL, the question you were trying to answer and the information you verified. Keep a distinction between a published fact and your own interpretation. “The page lists a language test requirement” is different from “I meet that requirement.” If two official pages conflict, contact the admissions office rather than asking an AI model to choose one. Saved account notes help you revisit the evidence later, but you must check it again before relying on a deadline or fee. Research access shown in a planned paid package should not be mistaken for an admissions guarantee.`, '/features'],
  email: ['Write a professional email with a clear next step', `A professional email normally needs less background than a formal application letter. Decide what you are asking for before drafting: a meeting, clarification, a document, a response or an update. State that purpose near the beginning, then include only the context the recipient needs. A clear subject line is useful when sending the message from your email application. The generator prepares text; you still control the recipient, subject, attachments and final send action in your own email tool.

For an admissions enquiry, identify the programme and the exact question. For a recruiter follow-up, mention the role and the date of your previous interaction if you know it. Keep your request polite and specific. Avoid language that pressures someone to respond immediately unless there is a real deadline you can explain. When reviewing the draft, remove unnecessary flattery and repeated introductions. Check that any referenced attachment actually exists and that any proposed meeting time includes the correct timezone. An email that is easy to act on often needs fewer words than a letter designed to explain your whole application.`, '/guides'],
  languages: ['Choose the output language deliberately', `The interface language and the writing language serve different purposes. You may browse the application in Russian while preparing an English university letter, or use the English interface to draft a French professional email. Choose the output language in the writing settings and inspect the result carefully. Company names, programme titles, qualifications and technical terms may need to remain in their official form. Translating them freely can make the application harder to verify.

Language quality depends on the source information and the generated response. The application does not promise that every sentence is suitable for every regional convention. If the letter is important and you are not confident in the language, ask a fluent reader to review it. Check greetings, levels of formality, punctuation and the meaning of claims about your background. Do not use translation to strengthen a qualification beyond what the original document says. PDF and text exports let you continue reviewing outside the application, but they do not replace a language check. A polished translation should preserve your facts and your intended meaning.`, '/features'],
  tone: ['Use tone to support the purpose', `Professional, formal, enthusiastic and concise tones are available as writing preferences. A formal tone can suit an institutional enquiry; a concise tone can suit a short follow-up. Enthusiasm should come from a specific reason for interest rather than repeated adjectives. Professional writing can still sound natural. Choose the preference that fits your reader, then add a short instruction if there is an important requirement, such as avoiding overly promotional language or keeping the message direct.

Tone is not a substitute for substance. Selecting formal will not correct an inaccurate programme title, and selecting enthusiastic will not create a credible example of your experience. Review the output sentence by sentence. Replace phrases that sound unlike you, remove claims that are too broad and keep the most useful evidence. If an employer or university provides style guidance, follow that guidance even if a different tone looks more impressive in a preview. Saved profile preferences are a convenient starting point, but you can change them for an individual application when the audience or context requires it.`, '/dashboard'],
  prompts: ['Give custom instructions that can be followed', `Good custom instructions describe a concrete constraint. “Keep this under 300 words, use a professional tone and focus on my customer support project” is easier to follow than “make this perfect.” Include the required points and anything the draft should avoid. For an application with a strict prompt, paste the relevant question rather than relying on a general description of the programme. If the document must include specific evidence, provide that evidence in your background and ask for its connection to the opportunity.

Use instructions to constrain exaggeration as well as style. You can ask the generator to use only supplied facts, leave out unknown names and avoid unsupported numerical claims. After generation, check that it followed those instructions. A language model can overlook a restriction or add a conventional sentence that does not fit your case. Rewriting the brief often solves a vague result more effectively than changing providers or repeating the same request. Keep useful instructions with the saved draft so you can understand why a particular version was written that way when you revisit it.`, '/guides'],
  editing: ['Review and edit before using a draft', `The review step is where an AI response becomes your document. Start by checking the basic facts: name, role, institution, dates, skills and qualifications. Then check the argument: does each paragraph help the reader understand why you are applying? Remove repeated claims, broad praise and sentences that could belong to anyone. Replace them with your own details where those details are relevant. A generated draft is editable in the workspace; you do not have to accept its original wording or order.

The draft review tools show word and character counts and can flag common placeholders. Those checks are practical editing aids, not a guarantee that the document is accurate or suitable. A letter can have the right length and still contain a wrong programme name. It can have no placeholders and still sound generic. Read the text aloud and compare it with the actual application instructions. If possible, ask another person to review the final version. The responsibility for submitting an honest and appropriate application stays with you, even when the first draft was prepared with AI assistance.`, '/features'],
  exports: ['Choose between PDF, text and copied content', `Use a text download when you want to continue editing in another application or paste the wording into a form. Use the copy action when the recipient expects text in an email or portal. Use PDF when the application accepts a document attachment and you want a stable visual layout. The available export does not decide what the recipient requires. Check the employer or university instructions before choosing a format, and preview the downloaded file rather than assuming it matches the on-screen appearance perfectly.

For long letters, inspect page breaks and confirm that the closing and signature remain readable. For non-Latin languages, check the characters in the actual PDF and make sure nothing is missing. Keep a sensible filename that identifies the application without exposing unnecessary personal information. Do not upload a file with another employer’s name in it. A downloaded copy is separate from the browser draft and the account copy; later changes to the editor do not automatically change a file you already downloaded. Export again after your final edits and attach the correct version.`, '/dashboard'],
  library: ['Organise saved letters and research notes', `A useful writing library makes drafts easy to identify. Give each item a title that includes the organisation or programme and the purpose, such as “Analyst role — autumn application” or “Masters programme — requirements notes.” The account dashboard supports saved letters, email drafts and research notes. You can search by title or content, reopen a draft, edit it and save the revised version. The cloud library currently displays up to 100 saved items; local browser sessions are a separate convenience.

Saving to the account is an explicit action. Signing in does not automatically upload every letter that already exists in browser storage. This distinction lets you choose which writing to keep across devices. Research notes should include the source and what you verified so that they remain understandable weeks later. Delete drafts you no longer need, and download an important final document for your own records. A saved draft is a working document rather than proof that an application was sent, accepted or reviewed by an employer. Use your own submission records to track those events.`, '/account'],
  profile: ['Reuse a profile without losing personalisation', `The account profile can hold your name, skills, experience, preferred output language and preferred tone. Reusing those details saves repeated typing, especially when you are preparing several applications. Keep the profile factual and current. A useful skills entry describes tools or capabilities you can demonstrate; a useful experience entry gives the setting and your contribution. Avoid putting passwords, identity document numbers, banking information or unrelated confidential material into the profile fields.

A profile is a starting point, not a universal letter. The opportunity still changes from one application to another, and the most relevant example may change with it. Use the profile in the generator, then select the background that fits the current reader. Review stored experience after a course, role or project ends so old information does not accidentally appear as current employment. If you share a device, sign out after using your account and remember that browser-local drafts may remain on that device. Account settings and browser storage serve different functions and should be managed separately.`, '/account'],
  allowance: ['Understand the shared plan allowance', `The planned Starter package costs $10 for a 30-day period and allows up to 30 standard letters, 8 premium letters or 5 university research requests. Plus costs $20 and allows up to 70 standard letters, 18 premium letters or 10 research requests. Pro costs $28 and allows up to 100 standard letters, 28 premium letters or 15 research requests. These are alternative maximums from one shared allowance. They are not three separate quantities that can all be used in full during the same period.

Mixed usage reduces the remaining allowance according to the operation you choose. A research request and a standard letter do not consume the same share. The account view shows the remaining practical maximums so you can decide whether another operation fits your available usage. The displayed quantities describe writing usage and are not a cash balance that can be withdrawn. Pro’s published limits are the final agreed limits; another 10 percent is not added again. Paid checkout remains unavailable until the paid generation flow is ready and verified. Read the current pricing page for the actual launch state.`, '/pricing'],
  starter: ['When Starter could fit your workflow', `Starter is the smallest planned paid package. It may fit someone preparing a limited number of applications or testing a more focused writing routine. Its listed 30 standard letters are the maximum for standard-only usage, while the 8 premium letters and 5 research requests are alternative maximums. If you expect to mix different operations, plan around the shared allowance instead of adding all three numbers together. Consider how many distinct applications you have and how much information is ready before choosing any package.

A person applying to a few well-matched roles may benefit more from careful preparation than from a large generation quota. Draft one application, review the evidence and refine the instructions before repeating the process. Editing an existing saved draft is different from requesting another AI generation. The planned price is $10 for a 30-day prepaid period, not an advertised annual price divided into twelve instalments. Check the checkout and renewal terms when the paid service launches. Until then, the published package describes the proposed offering rather than a promise that every paid operation is already available.`, '/pricing'],
  plus: ['When Plus could fit a larger application list', `Plus is the middle planned package at $20 for a 30-day period. The published maximums are 70 standard letters, 18 premium letters or 10 university research requests from a shared allowance. It may suit someone preparing more applications or a mixture of job and university writing. The decision should depend on the work you actually expect to complete during the access period. A larger package is not automatically better if your application details are incomplete or you only need a handful of final documents.

Map your expected usage before choosing: how many opportunities, how many first drafts and how many research questions require a fresh AI request? Keep editing separate from generation in that estimate. Saved documents can be revised directly without treating each keystroke as another generated letter. If a plan is later renewed or changed, inspect the current account dates and remaining usage rather than assuming a previous balance carries over under every circumstance. The pricing comparison explains the published package boundaries; the account shows your own recorded entitlement once purchases are enabled.`, '/pricing'],
  pro: ['Use the Pro maximums accurately', `Pro is the largest agreed package at $28 for a 30-day period, with maximums of 100 standard letters, 28 premium letters or 15 university research requests. Those published numbers already reflect the final plan decision. Do not expect an additional 10 percent to be added on top of them. As with Starter and Plus, the allowance is shared across operation types. A mixed month may produce fewer standard letters than the standard-only maximum because premium writing or research uses part of the same allowance.

Pro may be appropriate for frequent writing or a larger programme shortlist, but volume should not replace judgement. Recheck programme facts before sending multiple applications and keep employer-specific drafts clearly titled. A high limit is not a guarantee of better writing, interviews or admission. Premium and standard generation will need clearly described delivery behaviour when the paid backend launches; a label alone should not be used as evidence of a particular model or result. Choose a package for its verified capabilities and useful volume, then evaluate the final document on its content.`, '/pricing'],
  comparison: ['Compare prices on the same basis', `A fair subscription comparison starts with the billing period and what each product includes. A monthly plan, a 30-day pass and an annual subscription shown as a monthly equivalent are different commitments. A resume builder with job tracking and resume analysis also has a different scope from a focused letter writing service. Compare the advertised cost, the upfront payment, the available functions and the limits that matter to your own workflow. A lower sticker price is useful information, but it does not establish that two products are identical.

Our comparison identifies selected published plans and links to their providers so you can check current terms. The pricing research recorded Rezi Pro at $29 per month and Teal+ at $29 for 30 days. Our planned $10, $20 and $28 packages are lower than those particular listed options, with different usage limits and product scope. Free plans, discounted annual billing and promotions can change the decision. Do not read this as a claim that AI Letter Generator is cheaper than every service or has equivalent unlimited access. Review the comparison date and check the provider before purchasing.`, '/compare'],
  value: ['Evaluate value beyond the subscription number', `Value depends on what you need to finish. If you mainly need a cover letter and a professional email, a focused workspace may be easier to use than a broad career platform. If you need a resume builder, job application tracker, resume scoring or a full template library, a broader product may be worth its higher price. List your essential tasks before comparing subscriptions. Then test how each product handles your actual information, rather than relying only on an attractive sample written for an imaginary applicant.

Look at editing, exports, language handling, source checking and the ability to reuse your background. Inspect what happens when an AI response is inaccurate or a generation fails. Ask whether the package limits are separate allowances or alternative maximums from one pool. Consider the time you spend preparing and reviewing a draft as well as the purchase cost. No product can remove the need for accurate personal information and a final review. A useful comparison helps you choose a workflow that suits your needs, rather than telling you that one price alone settles every question.`, '/features'],
  free: ['Separate free use from planned paid access', `The current writing workspace and the planned paid packages must be described separately. An available editor, browser-local session or existing generation route does not mean that a new paid model tier is already operating. The pricing interface keeps checkout unavailable while the paid entitlement and generation delivery are being prepared. This avoids selling a package merely because the plan cards and payment provider integration exist. Look for the actual availability message in the pricing interface before making assumptions about purchasing access.

When comparing with another service, check its free tier separately from its paid plan. Free access may include different exports, generation counts or document types. A trial can also differ from an ongoing free option. Our comparison does not imply that users must buy a subscription somewhere else to draft a letter. If your immediate need is small, try the available workflow and inspect the result before deciding whether a paid package would provide practical value. Product descriptions should identify what you can use today and what is planned, rather than blur those states together.`, '/pricing'],
  failures: ['Know what to do when a request does not work', `A failed generation can come from a provider issue, an unavailable page, an unreadable upload or incomplete inputs. Keep your source information before retrying. Check whether the error concerns generation, university research, keyword extraction or saving a document, because those operations can fail independently. If the first response is simply vague, improve the brief rather than assuming there was a technical failure. A request that produces poor wording is different from a request that never completes.

If saving fails, keep a local copy of your text and try saving again after checking your connection. A message about an expired session means you need to sign in again before saving to your account. If the problem concerns a paid operation, check the actual account status rather than assuming that a success message on another page describes your current access. Contact support with non-sensitive context if a problem persists. Never send an API key, password or complete payment credential in a support message. Save the text you want to keep before refreshing a working editor.`, '/account'],
  limits: ['Understand what the application does not guarantee', `AI Letter Generator helps organise information and produce editable writing. It does not verify a qualification with an institution, submit an application to an employer, make an admissions decision or guarantee an interview. It does not turn a word count into a validated ATS score. A university summary can collect information, but the official institution remains the authority for requirements and deadlines. Understanding these boundaries makes it easier to use the service effectively and to recognise when you need advice from a person or a primary source.

The draft review tools provide practical checks such as length and common placeholders. Those tools cannot establish that every statement is true, that a programme accepts AI-assisted writing or that an employer prefers your chosen tone. You should make the final judgement with the actual prompt and your own records in front of you. If a decision depends on legal status, immigration requirements, scholarship eligibility or professional licensing, consult the relevant official authority. Use the application as an aid to drafting and organising questions, while keeping responsibility for the final document and its use.`, '/terms'],
  workflow: ['A focused workflow from context to final draft', `The product is organised around a simple sequence: prepare your background, add the opportunity, choose writing preferences and review the draft. This structure keeps the important decisions visible. You can provide a vacancy, an academic programme or a professional email purpose, depending on what you are trying to write. The guides explain how to create a brief for each situation, and the features page describes the actual tools available in the workspace. Internal links connect those explanations to the editor so you can move from reading to writing.

The account adds continuity across devices through an explicit cloud library and reusable profile. Browser-local sessions remain a separate convenience, so you can continue working without automatically uploading every historical draft. Pricing describes planned access levels, while the account reports your own stored payment and usage information. This separation matters: a marketing card should not be treated as a payment receipt, and a saved document should not be treated as proof of an application. The workflow aims to help you prepare a document you understand and can confidently review.`, '/features'],
  trust: ['Recognise useful product evidence', `A credible writing product should describe observable features and avoid unsupported success stories. You can inspect whether the editor works, whether the export opens, whether language settings affect the draft and whether a saved document can be reopened. Customer reviews can add another perspective when they come from real users who consented to publication. A decorative five-star rating or an invented user count is not evidence of reliability. Our landing page feedback area displays published, consented reviews when they are available.

The application does not invent testimonials to fill an empty section. Signed-in users can submit feedback with a public display name and publication consent. New or edited feedback waits for review before it appears publicly. A useful review can describe a particular writing task, an editing experience or a problem that needs improvement; it should not reveal private applicant information. Product claims should be assessed separately from individual opinions. Someone finding a draft helpful does not demonstrate that every user will receive an interview or that an admissions office will approve the same wording.`, '/about'],
  feedback: ['Share feedback without exposing an application', `If you want to review the service, describe your experience rather than copying a complete private letter. Mention the type of task, the part of the workflow that helped and anything that was difficult. For example, feedback about the ease of revising a draft is more informative than a short unexplained rating. Avoid naming a recruiter, publishing another person’s email address or sharing confidential details from a vacancy or application. The public display name can be different from the full name used in your writing profile.

The account review form asks for a rating, written feedback and explicit consent to publish the display name and text. Submission does not publish immediately. Reviews are stored as pending until they are checked, and editing a review returns it to that state. Only approved feedback with consent is shown on the landing page. There are no fabricated seed reviews and no aggregate rating presented without real data. If you want to discuss a private account issue instead, contact support rather than putting identifying payment information into a public review.`, '/account'],
  navigation: ['Find the right page for your next task', `Use the generator when you are ready to write. Use the features page to understand the tools, the guides page to prepare your information and the comparison page to understand the selected pricing references. The pricing page contains the agreed package limits and availability. The about page explains the product’s purpose and boundaries. Privacy and terms describe information handling and the responsibilities involved in using AI-assisted writing. Each page links to relevant next steps rather than leaving you at the end of an isolated article.

For personal documents, use the account dashboard after signing in. Its library, profile and plan view are separate from the public educational pages. Private account pages are excluded from search indexing; their purpose is to help you manage your writing, not to rank for a keyword. If you switch interface language, check the language of any explanatory article and the output language selected in the editor. Some long-form guidance is currently provided in English with an explicit notice. A locale URL should not be mistaken for a promise that every legal or educational paragraph has been translated.`, '/guides'],
  security: ['Protect the information you choose to enter', `Application writing often contains personal information, but it does not need every detail in your records. Provide what the task requires. A relevant skill, project or qualification may be enough; passport numbers, bank account details, medical records and account passwords do not belong in a letter generation brief. If a resume contains information you do not want an AI provider to process, remove that information before uploading or use a concise text summary instead. Check screenshots for private addresses and unrelated messages.

Account access is managed through Neon Auth, and protected account APIs verify the signed user identity before reading or changing stored writing. The server limits document operations to that identity. These controls reduce accidental cross-account access, but no online service can promise perfect security. Use a password you do not reuse elsewhere and sign out on shared devices. Keep downloaded letters somewhere you control. If you are unsure whether another person’s information may be included, obtain their permission or leave it out. Privacy-conscious preparation also makes a writing brief shorter and clearer.`, '/privacy'],
  dataflow: ['Understand the different places your data can exist', `There are several separate data flows in the application. Information entered in the writing form is used to prepare an AI request. Generated text can be kept in browser-local saved sessions. If you sign in and explicitly save a document or profile, that account information is stored in the server database. Downloaded text and PDF files are stored by your browser on your device. These locations have different purposes, so deleting or changing one copy does not automatically change every other copy.

For instance, editing a cloud draft does not rewrite a PDF you already downloaded. Signing out does not remove browser-local letters from a shared device. Saving a research note to your account is different from saving the raw resume upload. The account document flow stores the text and selected writing context; it does not automatically upload the whole local session history. When considering privacy, think about each copy you created and who can access that location. The policy and the interface should describe the actual storage behaviour, rather than claim that no data is stored when account saving is available.`, '/privacy'],
  minimisation: ['Provide the minimum necessary personal context', `A useful writing request normally needs a name or preferred signature, relevant background and opportunity details. It rarely needs a full postal address, a national identity number or detailed financial information. Before using the service, decide which facts the reader needs to assess your application. Replace unrelated personal details with a short description of the experience that matters. If you upload a document, inspect the entire file rather than just its first page, since old pages or comments may contain information you did not intend to share.

Data minimisation is also a writing technique. A shorter brief with strong evidence often produces a more focused letter than a long unfiltered history. In the profile, keep skills and experience relevant to the work you plan to do. In research notes, save the official URL and your verified observations instead of copying another applicant’s documents. In a review, use a public display name and omit confidential application details. These choices remain under your control and reduce how much unnecessary information appears in AI requests, saved writing and public feedback.`, '/privacy'],
  providers: ['Consider third-party processing separately', `The application uses third-party services for hosting, account authentication, database storage, AI processing and crypto payment handling when checkout is enabled. The current generation routes may use Puter and Google Gemini, depending on the available workflow. Neon provides authentication and database infrastructure, while NOWPayments is the integrated crypto payment provider. Sending a writing request to an AI service is different from saving a draft in the account library. Review the relevant provider’s terms and privacy information if their processing matters to your use case.

Do not assume that our interface controls a provider’s entire retention or training policy. Those practices can depend on the provider, account type and request route. The application should not claim that every provider deletes requests immediately unless that behaviour has been established. If you need a particular data-processing agreement or organisational approval, verify it before submitting sensitive work. You can reduce exposure by providing a concise factual summary instead of a complete document. Provider availability may change; use the current product behaviour and policy rather than relying on an old model name from an earlier planning discussion.`, '/privacy'],
  local: ['Manage browser-local drafts on shared devices', `Browser-local saved sessions help you return to previous writing in the same browser. They are not a private account vault and do not automatically synchronise across computers. Anyone who can use the same browser profile may be able to open locally saved drafts. Private browsing modes or browser cleanup can remove local storage, and changing browsers can make it look as if your drafts have disappeared when they are simply stored elsewhere. Keep an exported copy of work you cannot afford to lose.

The account library is an explicit alternative for documents you want to access after signing in on another device. Existing local drafts remain local until you choose to save their text to your account. To tidy up a shared browser, remove the local drafts you no longer need using the saved-session controls. Signing out of the account and deleting browser-local writing are separate actions. A downloaded PDF is another separate file. Understanding these distinctions prevents both accidental disclosure and mistaken expectations about recovery or automatic synchronisation.`, '/privacy'],
  credentials: ['Keep login and payment credentials private', `Your account password is used by the authentication service, not as part of a letter prompt. Never place it in custom instructions, a saved document, a review or a support message. The browser obtains a short-lived signed token for protected account requests, and the server verifies that token before accessing account data. A valid identity still does not authorise reading another user’s documents. Resource ownership is checked in the database operations as well as in the interface.

Crypto wallet access details, private keys and recovery phrases are unrelated to the application’s writing features and should never be shared with it. The payment integration creates provider checkout information without asking you to disclose a wallet recovery phrase. If a payment or account error appears, describe the problem with a non-sensitive order reference or screenshot that hides credentials. Public configuration such as the application’s auth service URL is different from a private API key. The application’s provider secrets belong on the server and must not be copied into public content or frontend fields.`, '/privacy'],
  deletion: ['Manage the copies you have saved', `The account library provides a delete action for a selected saved document. The server applies that action only to a document belonging to the signed-in user. Deleting a cloud draft removes that active library item; it does not delete a downloaded file or a browser-local session that you saved separately. If you want to remove those copies too, use the local saved-session controls and your device’s file management tools. Take care not to delete the only final copy of a document you still need.

Account-wide deletion requests are handled through support rather than an automated account-deletion button in this version. Contact support@ailettergen.com from the account email and describe the request without including your password. Payment records may need different handling from writing drafts, and provider or infrastructure backups are separate from the active library. This page does not promise an immediate universal purge across every third-party system. Ask about the specific information you want removed if you need clarification. Clear storage descriptions are more useful than a blanket claim that every copy disappears when a browser tab closes.`, '/privacy'],
  permissions: ['Use only information you are entitled to share', `A resume, screenshot or programme page can contain information about other people. You should have the right to provide the material you use and should avoid uploading confidential employer records, private messages or another applicant’s documents. If a useful example comes from collaborative work, describe your own role without claiming sole ownership of the whole result. If you quote a requirement from a vacancy, use it to explain the application rather than reproduce a large copyrighted document unnecessarily.

The same principle applies to generated writing. Review any named person, organisation, quotation or factual claim before sending it. The model may produce a plausible detail that was never present in your source material. Remove it unless you can establish that it is correct and appropriate to use. For academic applications, follow the institution’s rules about outside assistance and AI use. The service is intended to support honest writing from information you provide; it should not be used to impersonate another person or misrepresent an application.`, '/terms'],
  responsibilities: ['Keep responsibility for the final document', `Using an AI writing tool does not transfer responsibility for an application to the tool. You decide which background to provide, which draft to keep, what edits to make and where to submit it. You are responsible for checking whether the employer, university or other recipient accepts the chosen format and permits AI assistance. You should also check that the final text reflects your own experience and does not contain a claim you would be unable to explain or support.

A practical final check covers names, dates, qualifications, programme details, referenced attachments, contact information and any factual statement about the organisation. Remove placeholders and review language quality. If an instruction asks for a strict word limit or specific questions, check those requirements manually even if the draft review reports a convenient count. The application does not submit the document on your behalf or confirm that a recipient has read it. Keep your own submission records and use the recipient’s official process for deadlines, corrections or withdrawals.`, '/terms'],
  acceptable: ['Use the service for legitimate writing tasks', `The intended uses include preparing honest job application letters, university motivation letters and professional emails, together with related editing and research notes. Do not use the service to impersonate another applicant, fabricate credentials, send harassment, expose private information or prepare fraudulent requests. Do not attempt to access another user’s account or manipulate payment notifications to obtain usage you did not purchase. The fact that a model can generate a sentence does not make every requested use appropriate.

Keep your requests connected to your own legitimate task and use sources you are permitted to share. For academic work, respect the institution’s authorship and assistance rules. For professional correspondence, check that you have authority to speak for an organisation before writing in its name. For public reviews, describe your actual experience without publishing someone else’s private details. If a draft introduces harmful, false or inappropriate content, do not send it unchanged. Edit or discard it and reconsider the instructions that led to the response.`, '/terms'],
  access: ['Distinguish access, availability and delivery', `An account can provide a profile and a saved-writing library without an active paid plan. A planned pricing package can be displayed while checkout remains unavailable. A completed payment record is different from a waiting invoice, and a waiting invoice must not grant paid access. These distinctions help prevent confusion about what the service has delivered. Check the current interface messages and your account status rather than assuming that visiting a pricing page or opening a checkout creates a subscription.

The planned paid periods are 30-day prepaid access with the shared allowance described on the pricing page. Crypto payments are handled through the integrated provider when purchasing is enabled; they should not be described as an automatic card subscription. A browser redirect after checkout is a navigation event, not independent proof that payment is complete. The backend relies on verified provider status before recording fulfilment. If a purchase is pending or fails, do not send another payment blindly. Inspect the order status and contact support with a non-sensitive reference if necessary.`, '/terms'],
  attribution: ['Assess generated text rather than assuming originality', `AI-generated wording can be generic, resemble common templates or repeat phrases found in many applications. The application does not provide a plagiarism certification or a legal opinion on ownership. The safest practical approach is to make the draft specific to your experience, remove unsupported details and revise language that does not sound like you. A conventional greeting is not the same as a distinctive paragraph copied from someone else’s published work, but you should still review any content you intend to submit or publish.

Do not ask the generator to reproduce an entire paid template, private application or copyrighted article. Use a structure or a short description of the tone you want instead. If your institution has a disclosure requirement for AI assistance, follow it. If an employer asks you to prepare an original response without outside help, respect that condition. The service provides editable assistance and cannot decide every recipient’s rules for you. Your own review, accurate information and appropriate use are essential parts of the workflow.`, '/terms'],
  maintenance: ['Expect product details to be checked over time', `Product availability, model routes, provider pricing and university information can change. The application should distinguish a current feature from a planned one, and comparison pages should identify the selected plans and source links. A historical research note is useful context, but it is not proof that a price or requirement remains unchanged. Before relying on a comparison for a purchase or a research summary for an application, open the current official source and confirm the relevant detail.

The privacy policy and terms may also be updated to reflect changes in storage, account features or service delivery. Review those pages when a new feature changes the way you use the application. If a description appears inconsistent with the actual interface, ask support for clarification and avoid submitting unnecessary sensitive information while the issue is unresolved. This is a practical way to use an evolving service without treating old screenshots or planning discussions as a permanent product specification. Saved drafts remain your working text; they should be rechecked when the application context changes.`, '/about'],
  checklist: ['A final checklist before sending', `Open the actual application instructions and compare them with the document you intend to send. Confirm the correct recipient, role or programme, the deadline and the requested format. Read the first paragraph without the rest of the letter: does it identify the opportunity and give a specific reason for interest? Read each evidence paragraph separately: can you explain and support its claims? Read the close: is the next step polite, realistic and appropriate? Remove unnecessary repetition and any remaining template placeholders.

Next, inspect the exported file or the text pasted into the destination form. Check formatting, language characters, paragraph breaks and attachments. Make sure you have not selected a version meant for another organisation. Keep a copy for your own records and note the date and destination after submission. A letter generator can reduce the work of starting and revising, but the last check should happen in the actual context where the document will be used. A careful final review protects the value of all the preparation you have already done.`, '/guides'],
};

